## Project State Summary

**Problem:** go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development. It derives formal specifications from GNU C source code, generates Go implementations via an AI coding agent (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing. The goal is to find where the methodology breaks down.

**Architecture:** A four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) produces Go binaries in cmd/ that are verified against Homebrew GNU reference binaries via pkg/testutils. Shared infrastructure lives in pkg/sys (syscalls), pkg/format (output formatting), and pkg/testutils (differential testing harness). Build orchestration is in magefiles/.

**Current state:** 17 generation runs completed. 17 commands implemented (tee, head, seq, echo, tac, nl, fold, expand, unexpand, cut, paste, uniq, comm, md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum) plus infrastructure packages. All releases from 00.0 through 05.4 are marked "implemented". Code exists at generation-merged tags.

**Current release scope:** rel99.2 — the deferred ts utility. One use case remains: rel99.2-uc001-ts (spec_complete, code not started).

## Priority Analysis

Release 99.2 contains a single use case: ts (timestamp stdin lines). The spec is complete (PRD, use case, test suite all exist). The ts utility was part of the original rel01.0 but has been deferred to rel99.2. It needs implementation in cmd/ts/. Since I'm limited to 1 task and must split implementation from tests, I'll propose the implementation task. ts is a moreutils utility — the reference binary is `ts` from Homebrew moreutils (no `g` prefix).

```yaml
- index: 0
  title: "cmd/ts: implement ts utility (prd-ts R1-R4)"
  dependency: -1
  description: |
    deliverable_type: code

    required_reading:
      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd005-ts.yaml (full PRD for ts -- all requirements and acceptance criteria)
      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel99.2-uc001-ts.yaml (use case flow and success criteria)
      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-99.2.yaml (test suite for validation reference)
      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared_protocols section -- differential testing pattern, SIGPIPE handling)
      - cmd/tee/main.go (reference implementation of a simple streaming cmd/ utility for style and structure)
      - pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler usage)
      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests signature)

    files:
      - path: cmd/ts/main.go
        action: create
        note: ts utility implementation -- timestamp each stdin line
      - path: cmd/ts/ts_test.go
        action: create
        note: differential tests against Homebrew moreutils ts binary

    requirements:
      - id: R1
        text: |
          Implement the ts command that reads stdin line by line and prepends a
          timestamp to each line on stdout, per prd-ts requirements for default
          absolute timestamp mode. Default format is "%b %d %H:%M:%S" (matches
          GNU moreutils ts). Accept an optional format string argument that is
          passed to time.Format (converted from strftime to Go layout).
      - id: R2
        text: |
          Implement the -s flag for relative (elapsed) timestamps per prd-ts.
          When -s is set, timestamps show elapsed time since the program started
          in HH:MM:SS format instead of absolute wall-clock time.
      - id: R3
        text: |
          Implement the -i flag for incremental timestamps per prd-ts. When -i
          is set, timestamps show elapsed time since the previous line was
          printed, not since program start. -i and -s may be combined (both
          relative, but -i resets per line).
      - id: R4
        text: |
          Implement the -m flag for monotonic clock per prd-ts. When -m is set
          with -s or -i, use a monotonic clock source (time.Now() in Go is
          already monotonic-aware for elapsed calculations). -m without -s or -i
          has no effect. Write differential tests using pkg/testutils that
          compare output against the Homebrew moreutils ts binary. Use
          exec.LookPath("ts") with t.Skip if not found. Use timestamp
          normalization in DiffTest to handle timing differences.

    design_decisions:
      - id: D1
        text: |
          Call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main() per the
          shared SIGPIPE handling protocol in ARCHITECTURE.yaml.
      - id: D2
        text: |
          Convert strftime format specifiers to Go time layout equivalents
          in-process. The conversion function handles the common specifiers used
          by moreutils ts: %Y, %m, %d, %H, %M, %S, %b, %T, %s (epoch), %N
          (nanoseconds). Unrecognized specifiers pass through literally.
      - id: D3
        text: |
          The reference binary for differential testing is "ts" (not "gts") --
          moreutils does not use the g-prefix convention. Use exec.LookPath("ts")
          and t.Skip if not found, per the graceful skip protocol.
      - id: D4
        text: |
          Use testutils.TimestampNormalizer or a custom NormalizeFunc in DiffTest
          entries to mask timestamp values when comparing Go and reference binary
          output. Timestamps will differ by milliseconds between the two runs;
          normalize them to a fixed placeholder before byte comparison.

    acceptance_criteria:
      - id: AC1
        text: cmd/ts/main.go compiles with no errors via "go build ./cmd/ts/"
      - id: AC2
        text: "ts with no arguments reads stdin and prepends absolute timestamps in default format"
      - id: AC3
        text: "ts -s prepends relative elapsed timestamps in HH:MM:SS format"
      - id: AC4
        text: "ts -i prepends incremental per-line elapsed timestamps"
      - id: AC5
        text: "ts accepts a custom strftime format string argument"
      - id: AC6
        text: "Differential tests in cmd/ts/ts_test.go pass against the Homebrew moreutils ts binary (or skip gracefully if ts is not in PATH)"
      - id: AC7
        text: "go vet ./cmd/ts/ reports no issues"
```